&nbsp;
Hi James,
&nbsp;
Good to speak to you yesterday. As discussed, these applications were launched, and then heard, on an incredibly compressed timescale, at a time when
 there were i) a number of competing other critical path deadlines and ii) significant managerial changes and upheaval within PG. We apologise that the insurers were not put on notice sooner. On a positive note, and as per the attached, Counsel have advised
 that they do not feel that prospects of success have been materially altered as a result of these applications.&nbsp;
&nbsp;
To confirm, we need to try and reach agreement with the Defendants on these costs
today&nbsp;to avoid them being aired at the PTR on Monday.
&nbsp;
In terms of the timetable for when these applications were made to when judgement was handed down:&nbsp;

Defendants raised concerns over the content of the reports of Dr Heitz at the PTR on 29 July;Defendants crystallised those concerns in correspondences on 8 August;Claimants replied with detailed responses on 20 August;Applications were made by the Defendants (without response to our 20 August letter) on 27 August;Witness evidence for the applications was filed 2 September;Skeletons were filed 8 SeptemberHearing 10 September and oral judgement handed down 11 September.&nbsp;
&nbsp;
The timeframe for these applications also overlapped with finalisation of the supplemental expert reports and the substantive PDD Trial skeleton. We have since been provided
 with cost schedules by the Defendants for us to consider them with the schedules still being updated even to date. The costs sought by the Defendants are as set out in the below table, with PG's proportionate share of those costs in red:
