Alicia, this is our recommendation beneath. In short:

&nbsp;

a.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
We can’t totally ignore the case leads

b.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Tell LD (and counsel) the AA can’t be finalised before the PTR

c.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Speak to LD after the PTR to make necessary changes

d.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Then speak to case leads to tell them about changes, and for the rest they need to get with the picture

&nbsp;


The emails back and forth with this team is getting us nowhere. We think there needs to be a meeting with them, attended by Alicia and Anna. That obviously can’t be before the PTR, so would need to be on Tuesday
 at the earliest. But it might even have to be Wednesday. We would prepare you for this.
Their overwhelming issue is the delegation of work by LD to individual solicitors at PG. This has always seemed problematic to us, but LD would not budge. We presumed that in practice, things would continue to
 work as they have done up to now, and Martyn Day seemed to confirm that – but the language remains different.



To be clear, we are not conceding the principle that the case leads have the right to consent to this agreement – but they can’t just be ignored.



We consider (but should get it cleared by Clydes) that there is nothing inherently wrong or out of the ordinary with an employer telling an employee to do, for a limited period and for specific work, what a responsible
 third party tells it to do – as long as the employer continues to oversee and supervise (including in the SRA sense) the employee. So if the employer considers the employee is nor being directed properly, it can tell the employee not longer to follow the directions
 of the third party.
But that situation is not precisely what we have, because of the word “solely” in the Agency Agreement, where it relates to LD’s supervision of PG personnel doing the Common Work delegated to them. That
 makes it appear that only LD will know what the PG employees will do, so PG can no longer supervise them.
So what is the way forward? We suggest the following:



Tell LD (and counsel) now that we will not be able to agree the AA before the PTR, and that we will need to speak to them after the PTR. During that discussion, give them a summary of the case leads’ concerns,
 remind them that they want and need to work with the case leads, and the only way we can see this getting anywhere is if we drop the word “solely”. We must also tell them that (subject to Clydes agreeing this is acceptable) PG will let the employees
 know it is authorising them to do the work at LD’s direction on a limited basis (Common Work only), that PG will retain oversight and supervision, and if PG considers that any direction is improper (eg, a breach of regulations), it will have the right to rescind
 our instructions to the employees to follow LD’s directions. We see this as the only way forward. Since Natasha has already been in contact with Martyn, it may be that she needs to deliver this message.
At the same time, have Clydes look at the case leads’ letter and the agency agreement (with and without the word “solely”) and get their view on the employment law aspects (and how far we can push things). Clydes
 should also confirm that they see no employment issues with the way we are proposing to resolve this (which should not be an issue). Raminder can draft an employment response, but it should
not&nbsp;be sent until we have reviewed it and determined if it should be sent, consistent with our strategy.



When you meet the case leads on Tuesday (?), therefore, you will hopefully be able to say PG has amended the agreement, it is clear PG remains their supervisors, and you can be super-robust about everything else.



We would also suggest that after that meeting, you have a follow-up with them and Martyn Day, but we have to make sure Martyn is on side before suggesting that.



As for the PTR, there is no&nbsp;way we can agree the agency agreement (and any side letter) before then, so we will need to tell the court we’re in the final stages of agreeing it.
Ben Williams KC should be told that the agency agreement is being finalised –if necessary, he can say that is being done in consultation with the case leads. He can say that the case leads will be working their
 notice, which ends 15/19 Dec, but shouldn’t say anything more positive than that.
