Dear all Further to this afternoon's call, on the folder retitling, we thought it would be helpful to explain our take: We are conscious that the NOx Emissions Litigation has many aspects beyond the PDD Trial. Currently
 live issues include costs
&nbsp;
Dear all
&nbsp;
Further to this afternoon's call, on the folder retitling, we thought it would be helpful to explain our take:
&nbsp;

We are conscious that the NOx Emissions Litigation has many aspects beyond the PDD Trial. Currently live issues include costs management
 and preparation for the Quantum Trial. There are further matters of dispute, not yet listed for trial, which will become live in due course. The overall structure of the Opus workspace needs to enable all workstreams, and ideally do so in a way which avoids
 the need for constant reinvention.
&nbsp;

We think that the structure which has been in place for the last year or so has been working very well. We are wary of abandoning it or
 restructuring the Opus workspace in a way which focuses too closely on the PDD Trial to the exclusion of other workstreams.
&nbsp;

We are also conscious of the increasing complexity of the Opus workspace and would suggest that as a general principle it is preferable
 not to create additional bundles or folders if existing ones can be used. (Furthermore, if less critically, we are already over halfway through the alphabet.)
&nbsp;
In line with the above our strong preference is not to insert &quot;do not use&quot; to the titles of folders on Opus. We would suggest that &quot;GENERAL&quot; could be inserted in the titles, or for them to simply
 be left without an additional label. It should be sufficiently clear which are the preferred PDD Trial folders once &quot;PDD TRIAL&quot; is inserted to the relevant folders, particularly when read alongside the proposed guide to the bundle (which we think is an excellent
 idea). Titling a bundle as “Legacy” or “do not use” makes the Opus workspace more confusing for somebody needing to use it for workstreams other than the PDD Trial, and will mean that the Opus workspace needs to be restructured (again) when, in due course,
 the PDD Trial is complete and the next stage of the litigation begins. We suggest that Bundles A, H and J all fall into the category of “general” bundles which should be protected for use for all aspects of the NOx Emissions Litigation.
&nbsp;
There was also discussion on the call about reference being made to documents in Bundle A or a NOX bundle. To be clear, we agree with the approach to proposed standard references (and are grateful
 for the work which has been done on that front) and do not intend to refer to a document in Bundle A or a NOX bundle where it also appears in a specific PDD Trial Bundle. However we anticipate that there will (in practice, inevitably) arise the need to refer
 to documents which appear in Bundle A or a NOX bundle and nowhere else. Leaving the documents where they are and continuing to refer to them in A or NOx creates the least/no&nbsp;additional work for the Claimants. We would not want to move the documents
 from A or NOx at risk of losing notes/annotations to them. The alternative to leaving them in place will be for the Claimants to arrange slipsheets to the documents or for the Claimants to arrange with Opus for the documents to be re-uploaded in the PDD TRIAL
 folders (with our assistance), in doing so creating more duplicates in the bundle. While this might not be an issue for others presently, it seems preferable to leave the option available to refer to A or NOx should others find in the course of the trial that
 they also need to, rather than having to arrange slipsheets/new uploads and continuing to introduce duplicates to the bundle throughout the trial.
&nbsp;
We hope that these suggestions, which are intended to produce the most usable structure and avoid unnecessary future work, are agreeable; but of course we would be glad to hear others’ suggestions
 as to how best to accommodate the various workstreams and avoid over-complication.
&nbsp;
Kind regards
&nbsp;
Jess
